-- Curated, human-reviewed self-test — same set served at every level.
with q as (
  select $l23_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter says mission architecture is where 'the rocket equation stops being physics and becomes design.' What is the single principle underlying all good architecture?", "options": ["Always use the largest rocket available", "Never carry mass through a velocity change it does not need to experience \u2014 arrange the journey so each parcel of mass sees only the \u0394v it must, and no more", "Always land the whole spacecraft to keep the design simple", "Minimize the number of astronauts to save mass"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because the rocket equation is exponential, small differences in how a mission is structured produce enormous differences in launch mass \u2014 so architecture is where that leverage is tamed or unleashed. The taming principle, first met as staging, is to never carry mass through a \u0394v it doesn't need: drop empty tanks (staging), leave the return vehicle in orbit (lunar-orbit rendezvous), make propellant at the destination (ISRU). Each is the same idea applied to the whole shape of a journey, and it's why architecture must be chosen before any hardware \u2014 it sets the mass requirements for everything else."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does lunar-orbit rendezvous (LOR) let an Apollo mission fit on a single Saturn V, when direct ascent needed the never-built Nova?", "options": ["LOR uses a more powerful engine", "LOR leaves the heavy return vehicle (return propellant + heat shield) in lunar orbit, so only a stripped-down ~15 t lander is landed and only its ~4.7 t ascent stage returns \u2014 removing the most expensive mass (landed-and-returned) from the descent and ascent, a saving that compounds all the way back to the pad", "LOR skips lunar orbit entirely and flies straight to the surface", "LOR carries no return propellant at all"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The heavy Command and Service Module \u2014 the crew's ride home \u2014 never needs to touch the Moon. In direct ascent that whole ~45 t vehicle is decelerated onto the surface and re-accelerated off it, so its mass is multiplied by both the descent and ascent mass ratios; in LOR it simply waits in orbit while a minimal lander goes down and back. Because landed-and-returned mass is the most leveraged in the mission (it compounds through the rocket equation backward from the surface to the launch pad), removing it shrinks the required rocket dramatically \u2014 enough to fit one Saturn V instead of a rocket twice its size."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is 'mass that must be landed and returned' the most expensive mass in the whole mission?", "options": ["Because landing gear is heavy", "Because it compounds: it must be decelerated to land (descent \u0394v) AND re-accelerated to leave (ascent \u0394v), and both of those masses had to be braked into orbit, sent from Earth, and launched off Earth \u2014 each stage multiplying the mass by e^(\u0394v/v\u2091) again, backward from the surface to the pad", "Because the surface is far from Earth", "Because returned samples are scientifically valuable"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Follow one kilogram returned from the surface: ascent needs ~1.9 kg at liftoff; that had to be landed, another ~1.9\u00d7, so ~3.6 kg in lunar orbit; that had to be braked into orbit and sent from Earth and launched off it \u2014 each a further multiplication. So a single kilogram on the surface represents a compounding stack of propellant costs rippling all the way to liftoff. The rule this implies for any landing mission: land as little as possible, and return from the surface even less \u2014 which is why the Apollo lander even abandoned its descent stage on the Moon rather than lift the legs back up."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Reading the Apollo \u0394v budget (launch 9,400; TLI 3,100; LOI 800; descent 2,000; ascent 1,900; TEI 1,000 m/s), what two facts does the chapter draw out?", "options": ["Descent is the biggest cost, and the Moon has no gravity", "Launch to low Earth orbit is more than half the whole budget ('LEO is halfway to anywhere'), and there is no line for Earth-return braking because the atmosphere donates that huge deceleration for free", "Every maneuver costs the same, so architecture doesn't matter", "The budget is dominated by trans-earth injection"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Launch alone (~9,400 of ~18,200 m/s) is more than the entire round trip to the surface and back combined \u2014 the tyranny of Earth's gravity well, and why once you're in orbit you've spent more than half your total \u0394v. And the budget is only ~18,200 m/s because Earth's atmosphere brakes the ~11 km/s return for free (converting it to heat against the shield); without that gift the budget would nearly double. The \u0394v budget is the master tool because every architectural trick \u2014 staging, LOR, aerobraking, ISRU \u2014 either removes a line or keeps expensive mass off one."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Rendezvous made LOR possible but risky, and its orbital mechanics are counterintuitive. To catch up with a target ahead of you in orbit, what must you do \u2014 and why did NASA fly Gemini before Apollo?", "options": ["Speed up (thrust toward the target); Gemini tested engines", "Slow down \u2014 thrusting forward raises your orbit, which by Kepler's laws makes you move slower and fall behind, so you must drop to a lower, faster orbit to catch up from below; Gemini existed largely to prove rendezvous and docking in Earth orbit before betting lives on it at the Moon", "Fire sideways to change planes; Gemini tested spacesuits", "Wait passively for the orbits to align; Gemini tested reentry"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "In orbit, speeding up raises you into a higher, slower orbit and you fall behind \u2014 so to catch a target ahead you slow down, drop lower and faster, close from below, then rise to match. This is genuinely counterintuitive, and because LOR's whole success hinged on the ascent stage finding and docking with the waiting Command Module (fail, and the crew is stranded with no rescue), NASA could not risk trying it first at the Moon. The Gemini program de-risked it in Earth orbit \u2014 first rendezvous Dec 1965, first docking March 1966. LOR traded that catastrophic-but-managed risk for the decisive mass savings that met the deadline."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain how lunar-orbit rendezvous is 'the staging principle applied to the surface.' State the staging principle, identify what mass is 'dropped' (and to where) in LOR and why, and give one more example of the same principle from the course \u2014 including the Mars case.", "rubric": "THE STAGING PRINCIPLE (from Unit I / L3): drop mass the moment it is no longer needed \u2014 empty fuel tanks, spent engines \u2014 so you do not waste propellant accelerating that dead mass through the LATER velocity changes. Stated generally: never carry mass through a \u0394v it does not need to experience. LOR AS STAGING APPLIED TO THE SURFACE: the heavy Command and Service Module \u2014 carrying the return propellant, the Earth-entry heat shield, and the journey-home life support \u2014 is 'dropped' in the sense of being LEFT IN LUNAR ORBIT rather than landed, so it never experiences the lunar DESCENT (~2,000 m/s) and ASCENT (~1,900 m/s) velocity changes. Its return equipment is only needed for the trip home, not on the surface, so making it descend and re-ascend would be carrying mass through \u0394v it does not need \u2014 exactly what staging forbids. Only a stripped-down Lunar Module (no heat shield, no Earth-return propellant, minimal life support) descends and returns, and even it drops its descent stage on the Moon before ascent (no point lifting the landing legs back up). So LOR 'stages' the mission SPATIALLY: the part that doesn't need to make the trip down is separated and parked in orbit. WHY THIS SAVES SO MUCH: landed-and-returned mass is the most leveraged in the mission (it compounds through the rocket equation backward from the surface to the pad), so removing the ~30 t CSM from the descent/ascent saves far more than 30 t of launch mass \u2014 enough to fit one Saturn V instead of the never-built Nova. ANOTHER EXAMPLE / MARS: in-situ resource utilization on Mars is the same principle \u2014 rather than carry the return propellant all the way to Mars and land it (paying the full compounding penalty) just to launch it back, you MANUFACTURE it on the surface from the atmosphere's CO2 and the soil's water ice, so you never launch it from Earth, send it across space, or land it. (Also acceptable: dropping spent booster stages during launch; leaving the LM descent stage on the Moon; aerobraking, which lets the atmosphere 'pay' a braking \u0394v for free.) A strong answer names the unifying idea \u2014 never carry mass through a velocity change it does not need to experience \u2014 and shows it running from staging to LOR to Martian propellant."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Apply the architecture logic forward to a crewed Mars mission. Explain why the LOR-style insight is even more compelling at Mars, how the atmosphere and in-situ resource utilization each reshape the budget, why pre-positioning helps, and how it all ties to the through-line of the unit.", "rubric": "WHY LOR-STYLE IS EVEN MORE COMPELLING AT MARS: Mars has a DEEPER gravity well than the Moon and the journey is vastly longer, so the compounding cost of landed-and-returned mass is even MORE severe \u2014 landing the whole return vehicle on Mars and launching it back would be even more ruinous than at the Moon. So leaving the heavy return vehicle in Mars orbit and landing only a minimal vehicle saves even more; the Houbolt insight (don't land what doesn't need to land) applies with a vengeance. THE ATMOSPHERE \u2014 FREE DECELERATION: Mars has a (thin) atmosphere, so an arriving spacecraft can shed velocity by DRAG \u2014 aerobraking / aerocapture \u2014 instead of burning propellant to slow down. This is a large 'free' \u0394v (the atmosphere does the braking, converting energy to heat), impossible at the airless Moon where all deceleration must be propulsive. (The same atmosphere that complicates landing, by heating the vehicle, also donates the drag.) ISRU \u2014 THE MARS EQUIVALENT OF LOR: the return propellant is among the most expensive mass to carry, because (by the compounding logic) it must be launched from Earth, sent to Mars, AND landed, just to be used to launch the crew back. In-situ resource utilization MANUFACTURES the return propellant (methane + oxygen) ON MARS from the atmosphere's carbon dioxide and the soil's water ice, so you never launch it from Earth, never send it across space, never land it \u2014 breaking the worst of the rocket-equation compounding, exactly as LOR avoided landing the heavy return vehicle. PRE-POSITIONING: because a Mars mission is so massive, send the heavy pieces \u2014 return vehicle, habitat, ISRU plant, supplies \u2014 AHEAD of the crew on slow, fuel-efficient, uncrewed flights, so the crew departs lighter with a fuelled ride home already waiting; this splits the mission across multiple launches and years, dramatically reducing PEAK mass. (Robert Zubrin's 'Mars Direct' leans hard on ISRU + pre-positioning; other schemes add Mars-orbit rendezvous and aerocapture.) THE THROUGH-LINE: never send a human where a machine would do unless what the human brings is worth the enormous price \u2014 and mission architecture is the art of paying that price CLEVERLY, arranging the journey so the most demanding cargo there is (the human payload, L19) is delivered and returned through as few expensive velocity changes as possible. A strong answer stresses that every serious Mars architecture is the same exercise Houbolt performed for the Moon: structure the journey so mass is carried through each \u0394v only when it must be \u2014 and that the shape of the journey matters as much as the power of the engines."}]$l23_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
